> assume log means base 2
  ok: log means base 2
> what is the log of 8?
  ucas: log(8)/log(2)
3
> assume the log means base 10
  ok: the log means base 10
> what is the log of 100?
  ucas: log(100)/log(10)
2
> forget log
  ok: forgot log
> what is the log of 8?
  note: "log" without a base: read as the natural log, ln, not log base 2 or log base 10 (say "natural log", "log base 2" or "log base 10", or "assume log means base 2")
  ucas: log(8)
2.07944
> forget everything
  ok: forgot every assumption
> what is the log of 8?
  note: "log" without a base: read as the natural log, ln, not log base 2 or log base 10 (say "natural log", "log base 2" or "log base 10", or "assume log means base 2")
  ucas: log(8)
2.07944
> assume bytes are signed
  ok: bytes are signed
> what is the range of x for a byte x?
  ucas: it(x: i8) = x
  ucas: range it
range of it -> i8:
  -128..127   (256 distinct values)
  fits in i8
  over all 256 points of the domain
> forget bytes
  ok: forgot bytes
> what is the range of x for a byte x?
  note: "byte" without signed or unsigned: read as unsigned, u8, not signed, i8 (say "unsigned byte", or "assume bytes are signed")
  ucas: it(x: u8) = x
  ucas: range it
range of it -> u8:
  0..255   (256 distinct values)
  fits in u8
  over all 256 points of the domain
> assume and means bitwise
  ok: and means bitwise
> what is 6 and 3?
  ucas: 6 & 3
2
> assume and means logical
  ok: and means logical
> what is 6 and 3?
  ucas: 6 && 3
1
> forget everything
  ok: forgot every assumption
> what is 6 and 3?
  note: "and"/"or" between numbers: read as bitwise (& and |), not logical (&& and ||) (say "bitwise and" or "logical and")
  ucas: 6 & 3
2
